[{:seon.task/name "a-point-is-found-without-walking-the-branch"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `seon.ctx.wire/at-t` finds branch db's commit holding t by walking the line back from db, a ``datahike.api/commit-as-db`` a commit, on every call; `seon.system/back-point` calls it and keeps the commit when `(= t (:max-tx at))`. Measured 2026-11-11 (LMDB, Seon's layout, one-datom commits, the point at the first of n): 379k, 4.17M and 42.1M engine ticks, 1.7, 18 and 186 MB, at n = 10, 100 and 1,000 (42k ticks and 185 KB a commit). What replaces it: ``datahike.versioning/commit-at-tx`` (Datahike pin `f47067b5`, docs/storage.md#a-points-commit): `(datahike.versioning/commit-at-tx db t)` answers `{:branch :commit :t}`, the commit of db's lineage whose last transaction t' is the greatest <= t, exact when `(= t (:t p))`; `(datahike.versioning/commit-holding-tx db t)` the commit whose span holds t, which is what at-t answers now (its earliest commit holding t). Both read each commit once per process and keep it in memory, then confirm the answer with one ``konserve.core/exists?`` (9.0k ticks): measured over the same span, a first lookup 169k, 1.69M and 16.9M ticks (one commit-record read, 16.8k ticks and 64 KB, a commit walked, plus the exists?), a repeat 9.4k (the exists? alone), k reads and the exists? after k new commits. What bounds the first walk: it stops at the first commit whose last transaction is <= t, so a resume from a recent point reads few commits and one from an old point many (a 1,000-commit-old point: 1,000 record reads). The commit graph record would read cheaper (12.6k ticks, 40 KB) but holds no max-tx; carrying the walk on it would store a derivable value, not done (owner, 2026-11-11), the number recorded for him. Change: back-point takes `(datahike.versioning/commit-at-tx db t)` and keeps `:commit` when `(= t (:t p))`, else refuses as now; at-t becomes `(d/commit-as-db (:store db) (:commit (datahike.versioning/commit-holding-tx db t)))`, and where a sweep took the commit the refusal `{:type :commit-expired :branch :tx}` (ex-info at its named edge) gives `(d/as-of db t)` as now; `:tx-after-head` and `:tx-before-first-commit` are a caller's error, never a nearby commit. Delete the walk loop."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.ctx.wire-test/a-point-reads-each-commit-once}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-task-is-shown-once"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: an agent's task (its plan, proofs, cites) is in `@ctx` and drawn again whole on the model's page, so the first request holds it twice. Plan: the task whole is in `@ctx`; the model's page shows one line naming it and what is derived from it (its proofs' states, what its cites resolve to), never the plan's text again (docs/ctx.md#what-the-model-reads-and-what-the-page-renders). Deletes: the page's copy of the plan. Proof, red first: in a seeded agent's first request the plan's text appears once; a recorded request moves by exactly that part."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.ctx.wire-test/the-plan-is-in-the-first-request-once}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-call-seon-makes-is-known-by-its-function-not-its-text"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-12 (batch 84 red): the calls Seon makes for an agent ((my.agent/describe @ctx), (my.render/page p), (my.render/diff p0 p1)) are written once, by seon.ctx.wire/page-message, as code text, and read back by matching that text: seon.judge/request found the first point's record by the prefix \"(my.render/ctx \", so when the first point became (my.agent/describe @ctx) the judge keyed every pass anew (seon.judge-test/a-pass-scores-each-request-once red). Readers by text, swept with git grep: src: seon.judge/request (fixed to the describe call's text, still text); test: seon.fixtures/two-calls and the scripted model beside it (\"(my.render/\" or \"(my.agent/describe \" prefixes), seon.ctx.wire-test's page-call?, seon.agent-test's page? (twice), and the asserts that compare the code strings. The change: the call has one definition, the function it names as data: page-message records it on the call it writes (the var, beside :code), and every reader asks that (one predicate in seon.ctx.wire, a Seon-made call and which), never the text. Proof: changing the first point's function in one place leaves the judge's key and the fixtures right; a reader that matches the text is red."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.ctx.wire-test/a-call-seon-makes-is-known-by-its-function}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-turn-is-a-component-of-the-request"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: the agent's model render, seon.ctx.wire/agent-msgs, places every turn's entities itself (turn-entities: the placed messages, the page pairs, each call and its evals), so any change to the conversation's key set (one eval more) runs it whole again, ~243k ticks at 40 turns, and each of its placed values is a reused component looked up again: seon.render/walk's one eval more costs 420k ticks plus 17k a turn (1.11M at 40; seon.render-cost-test/a-request-made-again-costs-what-moved). The cause: a turn is not a component of the request. Plan: agent-msgs places one call a turn ([#'turn-msgs {} n] with what that turn reads), so one eval more runs its own turn's call and the root's list of calls, each other turn reused by its arguments; the lower bound is that turn's render and the messages put together. Found by Web, 2026-11-12, measuring seon.render/walk. Proofs: that cost proof's estimate made constant in turns (its :per-n gone), and seon.ctx.wire-test's request proofs unchanged. Agreed with web 2026-11-14: this lands first, one call a turn ([#'turn-msgs {} n] in agent-msgs); then web's W-3 (seon.render/assembly-costs-what-changed) keeps each turn's assembled messages by rev, and together they remove the ~156-161 KB a request made again spends re-listing every turn [M, web]. The :overrun waiver deps-2's landing holds on that test is removed by this task. The bound, agreed with web 2026-11-14 (the owner: \"How can they have no idea what the bounds are?\": no waiver, no restating). [M, web] seon.render-cost-test/a-request-made-again-costs-what-moved measures 2,533 / 4,173 / 7,545 calls at 10 / 20 / 40 turns: ~168 calls a turn plus ~850 calls and ~1.8 MB fixed. The fixed part is the work a request made again must do: the new eval's component (its response printed once, ~600 calls), its call's, the turn's page message. So the bound is constant in n: this task makes each turn one component in agent-msgs ([#'turn-msgs {} n]), so a new eval reruns its turn alone; web's W-3 keeps the turns list's answers by rev and appends (the conversation grows only at its end), so the request is a kept persistent vector and a new turn's messages are conj'd. The proof becomes :growth :constant, fixed ~850 calls and ~1.8 MB; an edit of an earlier turn (an unlink, a compaction) costs that edit, still nothing per turn."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.render-cost-test/a-request-made-again-costs-what-moved}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-turns-page-message-is-read-from-the-one-walk"
  :seon.task/plan "W-2 (context's, filed from root's notes 2026-11-14). Web's W-4a is on main (c19d3f7e81): the agent's walk is a render/walk value in its state (seon.repl/page-walk), wire/request takes [p db] and reads the answers' sources, and a cold request walks :once, so the second walk below can go. What exists. Each turn's page message is a component the agent's model render places per turn, `[#'page-diff-msgs opts a p0 p1 n]` (wire.clj:490): it diffs the page parts at bases p0 and p1, which come from a second walk, `page-parts-through` (:363): a walk of its own (`parts-walk` :337, rooted at `model-parts` over `page-ctx`, a pull without the conversation's joins), kept in agent/state's one slot and advanced point to point by wire's own `advance` (:348); `pages-from` (:461) shares it across a cold request's pairs. Web's walk (9434c6e308) already draws the same page components each turn, with `:answers` keyed by component and `:ran`. Cause of the duplicate. The model's page parts and the person's page are drawn by two walks over two pulls of one agent; the second exists because the request needed past points' pages and the first walk only drew now. Design. One walk value per owner draws the agent with both outputs. The page parts at a point are that walk's hiccup output of the model-parts components (`model-parts` stays, as a component of the one tree, not a root). A pair's two pages are the walk at p0 and at p1: live, the walk the agent's proc keeps, moved turn to turn (`:moved` from the commit, O(change)); replay (the debug page, the context op), the same walk advanced through the recorded bases in order (`seon.render/walk` with `:moved` between points, as pages-from does today, but the one walk). The diff stays render/page-edits over forms-moved; which parts moved is `:ran`. Deletes (same landing): page-parts-through, parts-walk, wire's advance, page-ctx, pages-from, the ::parts-walk slot in agent/state, page-edits-between's second draw; render.clj's per-pair re-draw if unused after. Keeps: page-message, diff-reply, describe-msgs, page-diff-msgs (its body reads the one walk), editscript diffs (B13's placement stays a config question). Proof, red first. seon.ctx.wire-test: a turn's page message is made from the agent's one walk: no second walk is drawn (a cold request of 3 turns draws its tree once and moves it twice: a call count); the bytes of every turn's messages equal today's (the prefix holds: seon.render-test/the-page-and-the-request-agree and the sent-hash test stay green). Estimate. Live turn: the page parts that moved, O(change), about 1-5M ticks. Cold replay of n turns: one cold draw plus n-1 moves, about 2.5M + n x 1M on session-of's world. src about -180 / +60 in wire.clj. Risk. Replay bytes must equal what was sent (the prefix check, seon.turn/cache-miss): checked by the existing sent-hash test."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.ctx.wire-test/a-turns-page-message-is-read-from-the-one-walk}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-agents-system-message-carries-the-principles"
  :seon.task/plan "Filed from root's notes 2026-11-14 (root's note of 11-13, not built). What exists. The system message is the wiki sections the agent's record names (`:seon.agent/system`, seon.ctx.wire/agent-system-text), else the system prompt page; it is byte-identical across agents (2.5 KB measured), first in every request, so the provider caches it. The carrying. principles.md becomes one of those sections, named by default for every agent (the config's default `:seon.agent/system`), so it is read once per agent at the head of the cached prefix: no per-turn cost after turn 1, shared across agents by the provider's cache (r1 sec. 8: the system message is the one part provably the same). What an agent needs from it, and what it doesn't. An agent works one task: \"boring by construction\" (the one sanctioned place per risk), \"a live REPL platform\" (try small, read your result and your reasoning) and \"raise the alarm\" (a second mechanism, a guard, a wait on a clock: say so in its reply, which reaches its starter) are its business. \"How designs are chosen\" is for sessions building Seon; it costs every agent tokens on every run. So: the page's agent-facing sections go in the system message; the design rules stay a section an agent reads on demand (`(doc \"docs/principles.md#how-designs-are-chosen\")`). Raise the alarm, as data. An agent's alarm is a message to its starter (seon.message) with a schema'd body (``:seon.alarm/rule``, ``:seon.alarm/evidence``), so root and the review task read alarms by query rather than by reading replies. Measure. The system message's tokens before and after, against the 512-1,024-token minimum cacheable prefix (agent-context.md 1.1); the cache-hit ratio per turn must not fall (today 97-98%). Open. Whether alarms need their own schema or are a :seon.task/failure variant; which sections are \"agent-facing\" is the owner's call."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.ctx.wire-test/an-agents-system-message-holds-the-principles-agent-sections}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-cold-request-makes-no-past-point-read"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: context. THE PLAN item 16, the slice after the first (jolt/context bbb8ccd741: each turn stores its page messages, :seon.part/digest and :seon.part/message by canonical digest, and its call's :seon.model-call/assembly; seon.ctx.wire/stored-page reads them). Left: (1) a request made again at turn n's basis still draws its own pair once (p0 is the previous basis: a read of a past point), since its assembly is written after its request; a debug redraw or a cold restart can read turn n's assembly too once it exists, so only the request being made draws, at its own head. (2) Once every live agent's turns hold assemblies (a turn before the first slice has none), delete seon.ctx.wire/page-parts-through's past-point chain, pages-from and db-at's as-of fallback; a turn with no assembly and a swept commit answers :seon.ctx/point-swept, never an as-of read. (3) Measure the cold request again on a real agent of 30+ turns after a round (today 11B ticks / 37.6 GB at 30 turns, result/a444fa99; estimate under 50M). Proof: seon.ctx.wire-test/a-cold-request-makes-no-past-point-read."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.ctx.wire-test/a-cold-request-makes-no-past-point-read}
  :seon.task/estimate #:seon.ctx.wire{render {:n 30, :order :linear, :ticks 50000000, :bytes 100000000}}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-walks-model-output-costs-what-it-draws"
  :seon.task/plan "Context's (asked web to file it). The walk's model-output path: drawing :seon.render/openai-compat for root's session page (kept-walk on :main/task-request-root, 506,583 datoms) costs 6.8B ticks / 35.8 GB cold against the page hiccup's 163M / 0.95 GB on the same walk [M result/b9a43662]. Profile [M result/d7186cdc]: seon.render/walk's own uncontracted code ~6.8B / 35.8 GB; ctx.wire/page-diff-msgs (7 calls) 223M and page-parts-through 221M are small, so it is not the past-turn pairs; search/vector ~0.6B. Every cold start of an agent's own page and a debug view of root's session pay it (a debug view is one 6.8B render-proc step that every page waits behind). Context measure!s the inner steps first, then plans. Baseline to beat: 6.8B; landing 5's stored sent parts estimate ~7M at 144 turns.\n\nCites: `seon.render/walk`, `seon.ctx.wire/request`, `docs/render.md#a-page-is-a-kept-walk`. The proof's name is context's to settle."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/estimate #:seon.ctx.wire{request {:n 144, :order :linear, :ticks 7000000, :bytes 20000000}}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.ctx.wire-cost-test/a-cold-request-costs-what-its-model-output-draws}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-turns-stored-parts-cost-what-the-turn-added"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: context. Root's review of landing 5 (67966fb6aa), item 1: the per-turn write was never measured. MEASURED [M root result/c95f574a, da6a43cf, ddea4ea3; agent l582a76f4eb66eb1's turn 40: 151 messages, 279k chars]: seon.ctx.wire/sent-tx 1.8M ticks / 19 MB a turn against an estimate of ~50k (36x: the gap is the finding); its cost was pr-str escaping every message's content to digest it (1.26M / 15 MB); seon.ctx.wire/part-digest (content raw, length-prefixed; the rest canonical EDN) took it to 0.98M / 9 MB (jolt/context, lands with request!), still O(N) in the request a turn. CAUSE: every turn digests every message it sends, where the design's estimate assumed O(d), the parts the turn added. DESIGN, to settle before code: reuse the digest of each part the previous call's assembly holds unchanged, so a turn digests only what it added; the open question is how to know a part is unchanged without hashing it (the kept walk returns identical message objects for unchanged components, so identity is a candidate; a ref and turn alone are not, since an opened eval re-renders under the same ref). Lower bound: the turn's new messages' bytes once. PRIOR WORK: seon.ctx.wire/sent-tx, assembly-parts, part-digest; seon.render's kept walk (seon.repl/page-walk). COST: O(d) a turn, ~50k ticks at a few new messages. Proof: seon.ctx.wire-cost-test/a-turns-write-costs-what-it-added (a turn adding k messages to an N-message request costs O(k), constant in N). BUILT (jolt/context, 2026-11-15): identity it is. seon.ctx.wire/sent-digests takes a digest again only where the request holds a message not identical to the one at its place in the request before (seon.turn/request! keeps the last request's messages and digests in the agent's state, the REPL-state exception principles 5 names); sent-tx takes the digests and digests each new message once (it digested each twice). Proof red first: seon.ctx.wire-test/a-turn-digests-only-the-messages-it-added (one changed and one added message of six: 2 digests; red with the reuse off). OWED: the live number on a real agent's turn after the land (estimate ~50k ticks at a few new messages, against 0.98M today).\n\n2026-11-16: still owed. How to measure: at root's REPL, take an agent's last two model calls (:seon.model-call/agent, :seon.model-call/n) and measure seon.ctx.wire/sent-tx for the newer with seon.cost/measure; compare with ~50k ticks. Root's cold request, measured the same way stage by stage (db-at, program/index-of-db, ctx/agent-ctx, render/walk with :once, wire/request), is 11.1 GB after 9fb813b20e [result/de815dbd]. Web's server-op bound (:seon.config.fx/bounds, jolt/web 748c041a4e) is arithmetic: ~1k ticks and ~1 KB a request char, 10x that is the bound. Before 9fb813b20e the cold request was ~11k ticks and ~39 KB a char [result/a444fa99]."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.ctx.wire-test/a-turn-digests-only-the-messages-it-added}
  :seon.task/estimate #:seon.ctx.wire{sent-tx {:n 150, :order :constant, :ticks 100000, :bytes 1000000}}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-stored-turns-page-keeps-what-a-later-fix-removed"
  :seon.task/plan "Context's (filed by web, evidence measured 2026-11-16 by seon_context on root's session): root's request is 746k chars after web's fix landed (main ba730d71f2, a thrown component's error no longer prints a walk's index), because root's FIRST turn's page is replayed as it was sent (the stored parts of context's landing 5) and that stored page holds the 500k-char index printed by a thrown seon.render.ui/searched (sqlite ranked: out of memory). New draws are clean; the stored one stays until the conversation moves past it. Decide: a fold or refine that drops the first page from what the model is sent, or a fresh conversation for root's session; and whether a stored part found to hold a defect's dump is ever re-drawn. Also yours: the sqlite ranked query's out of memory in seon.render.ui/searched."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.ctx.wire-test/a-past-turn-reads-back-as-it-was-sent}
  :seon.task/estimate #:seon.ctx.wire{request {:n 1, :order :linear, :ticks 100000, :bytes 1000000}}}

 {:seon.task/name "a-turns-page-diff-reads-the-live-walk"
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-16 by web, measured at fresh agent o606329cf380fa3c (seon.ratom/web-reads-on-the-core): after one plain eval, seon.ctx.wire/page-diff-msgs costs 2.9 MB, the largest component a turn's walk reruns. Cause, read: for a new turn's pair (p0 the last basis, p1 the new) it draws the page at p1 by advancing its own kept model-parts walk (page-parts-through, ::parts-walk in seon.agent/state), strips presentation and diffs (render/page-edits over forms-moved): a second walk of the same page beside the agent's live page walk (seon.repl/page-walk), whose parts at the newest point it already holds. PRIOR WORK: page-parts-through and pages-from (wire.clj ~380, ~478), stored-page, seon.repl/page-walk, render/page-edits. CHANGE: page-diff-msgs takes the parts at p1 from the live walk when p1 is its current point, and the parts at p0 from what the last turn kept (stored-page, or the live walk's previous value); the parts walk serves only past points (a cold request, the debug page). Deletes: the per-turn advance of the second walk. BOUND: a turn's page message is the edits of what moved, KB; its cost O(moved parts), no walk of its own. MEASURE first at an agent: page-diff-msgs' bytes and ticks a turn, twice warm. Proof, red first: seon.ctx.wire-test/a-new-turns-page-diff-draws-no-second-walk (the new pair's diff equal to today's, the parts walk not advanced). FOUND BEFORE BUILDING (context with web, 2026-11-16): not a swap. Two shapes both say they are the page as the model reads it: (a) presentation-free hiccup of the whole page under one id \"page\" (page-parts-through, page-parts-at, page-edits-between: what my.render/page and my.render/diff answer and page-diff-msgs writes); (b) seon.render/part-form per part, {id form} in order (seon.render/placed-hiccup, documented as the form the model reads; the render proc and the task page use it). Measured at agent p8edf9ac [result/c222e3ce]: page-diff-msgs 1.47 MB and 0.28M ticks a turn, 1.38 MB of it the second walk; private pages per eval +0.2-0.3 MB, so this is ticks, not the 5 MB row. web: (placed-hiccup (seon.repl/page-walk) [\"#p-task\"]) answers 9 parts in 0.27 ms, 338 KB, from the live walk's cache, re-running nothing. PLAN (data model first, one shape): the model's page is (b); my.render/page and my.render/diff answer {id form} and edits over forms-moved of (b); a turn's newest point's parts come from the live walk (placed-hiccup), the previous point's from what the last turn kept or stored (stored-page); a past point (a cold request, the debug page) draws one walk with :place at it. DELETES: shape (a), presentation-free's whole-page diff, page-parts-through's per-turn advance. CHANGES WHAT EVERY AGENT READS (the page and its diffs, recorded requests): root's go-ahead before code. BUILD (agreed with web and root, 2026-11-16): DONE on jolt/context: one shape (part-form), model-parts/page-ctx/presentation-free deleted, page-lists honours a list var's :wire/for, a past point drawn as the live walk draws it; 4 real pages compared, every difference intended (the agent's own cites and recent-failures now read; wrapper ids gone) or fixed; recorded requests byte-identical. TO DO, root's bar (per-turn ticks and bytes at or below today's, old against new on the same turns; ::parts-walk deleted in the same landing): page-diff-msgs runs inside the live walk, so the newest point's parts can't be read from it mid-run (a cycle; web: no cleaner seam). So: turn-entities stops placing opening-page; seon.ctx.wire/request makes the opening page message from the walk value it is handed (after the turn's last advance, web's condition): h1 = (point-parts p), h0 = the parts kept from the last turn in seon.agent/state {:point :parts} when its point is the last call's basis, else one cold draw at that point (parts-walk, kept nowhere); the message keeps its [:page n] part ref so sent-tx and stored-page hold it; earlier turns read stored-page. Deletes ::parts-walk, advance, pages-from's chain draw for the live path. MEASURE: page-diff-msgs per turn today 1.47 MB, 0.28M ticks [result/c222e3ce]; web's placed-hiccup 0.27 ms, 338 KB. Web takes seon.repl/render-ctx-hiccup and the debug label right after. MEASURED 2026-11-16 at agent a740942f5f6a6ba3 on the worktree system (jolt/context 646b978d9f), RED against the bar: opening-msgs with the kept parts matching p0 costs 0.60M ticks and 3.7 MB a turn (result/c40d9071, less the eval floor 33k ticks, 0.47 MB, result/a1655f9d), point-parts alone 67k ticks, 0.5 MB (result/a1760548); but in a real session the kept point never matches: ::last-page is keyed by the request point p1, and the next turn's p0, the last call's basis, is later (the request's own record, sent-tx, is written after it: replies showed p1 t=536874652 then p0 t=536874663), so every turn draws p0 cold, 800 ms, 8.9M ticks, 23.5 MB (result/a3523a36 setup), 45x the 200 KB bound: the regression a-flush-between-turns-leaves-the-next-turns-pair-to-advance was written for (the old walk kept the chain's first point and advanced). NEXT, a design choice: (a) diff from the kept point and label the message with it (what this process last drew; wrong for a model when a :context read drew in between), or (b) keep the live walk's parts at the basis when the call is recorded, so p0 always hits. SECOND DEFECT FOUND (not this task's; owner to be named): the live @ctx holds the agent's 4 cites (begin-run!'s agent-cites-tx, seon.agent/transact!) that its branch db never held, in history either (result/a706636e, result/a0c32fcb; root's main holds none, result/d515bcbd): the live page shows them, a cold draw does not, so each turn's diff re-adds all 4."
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.ctx.wire-test/a-new-turns-page-diff-draws-no-second-walk}
  :seon.task/estimate {seon.ctx.wire/page-diff-msgs {:n 10, :order :linear, :ticks 500000, :bytes 200000}}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-parts-walk-keeps-no-db"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: context. FOUND (web, 2026-11-16, result/a3148031): an agent's state holds :seon.ctx.wire/parts-walk with :db a datahike db value 157 transactions behind its branch's head, kept between turns. A kept db pins its version of the index (docs/storage.md#a-kept-db-value-is-a-reference); one replaced value, not a growing leak, but against the rule and a suspect in deps' agent-death hunt. THE CHANGE: the parts walk keeps its db as a point ({:branch :commit :t}, seon.db/point) or reads the agent's head where it is drawn. PROOF, red first: after two turns the agent's state holds no db value under ::parts-walk."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.ctx.wire-test/the-parts-walk-keeps-no-db}}
]
